<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kunene River</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Cunene River</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kunene, or Cunene, is a river of south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> that rises in the Angolan highlands and flows south and then west to the Atlantic, forming part of the border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namibia">Namibia</a>. Its floodplains supported the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ovambo-people">Ovambo</a> and Nkhumbi kingdoms. The Ruacana and Epupa falls lie on its lower course, and its waters are used for hydroelectric power.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kunene-river.jpg" alt="Kunene River" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pavel Špindler · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%C5%98eka_Kunene_River,_vodop%C3%A1dy_Epupa_Falls_-_Namibie,_Angola_-_panoramio.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
